INT. WINE-SOAKED FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - 1944 - NIGHT

A cold stove. A table with two chairs. A candle in a saucer. Far off, the rolling thud of a front that is slowly moving away.

SERGEANT EUGENE PAYSON (30s, GI, dirt on his cheek, his helmet on the table) sits across from MADAME ODILE FAUCHEUX (60s, widow, black shawl, a ladle clutched in one hand like a scepter).

PAYSON
You've got a nice place, ma'am.

ODILE
It is not nice. It is standing.

PAYSON
Same thing, these days.

ODILE
You are hungry?

PAYSON
Is it that obvious?

ODILE
Your stomach has been speaking to me since you entered.

PAYSON
I'm sorry. I'll tell it to hush.

ODILE
It does not hush. I have a husband of the same nature. He does not hush either.

PAYSON
Where is he?

ODILE
In the orchard.

PAYSON
Working?

ODILE
Buried.

PAYSON
I'm sorry.

ODILE
Do not be. He was a very loud man, and now I have the quiet I always wanted.

PAYSON
(to himself)
Lord.

ODILE
You pray?

PAYSON
Not in a long while.

ODILE
(to the room)
Then someone should.

She sets down the ladle. Ladles stew into a chipped bowl anyway. Sets it before him. He stares at it.

PAYSON
Ma'am, I can't. You've got nothing.

ODILE
I have a pot of something and a young man who has not eaten. This is not nothing.

PAYSON
Ma'am--

ODILE
Eat.

He picks up the spoon. The first bite closes his eyes. The thud outside drifts farther away.

PAYSON
(softly)
That's the best thing I've had in a year.

ODILE
It is turnips.

PAYSON
It's the best turnips I've had in a year.
